L000748	\section{Fluctuating hydrodynamics and gradient expansion}
L000749	\label{FHD from derivative expansion}
L000750	In this section, we systematically construct the fluctuating hydrodynamics from the microscopic Hamiltonian. The construction proceeds by identifying conserved quantities, introducing the corresponding hydrodynamic variables, and writing down all terms allowed in a gradient expansion subject to the symmetry constraints, following the philosophy of effective field theory.
L000751	
L000752	\subsection{Fluctuating current in the hydrodynamics}
L000753	We consider an $N$-particle system in one dimension, described by the Hamiltonian
L000754	\begin{align}
L000755	H &= \sum_{i} \frac{1}{2} p_i^2 +  \sum_{i } V(q_{i+1} - q_i), \label{supplhamil}
L000756	\end{align}
L000757	where $p_i$ and $q_i$ are the momentum and position of the $i$-th particle, respectively. Here, $i \in {\mathbb Z}/N {\mathbb Z}$, and we consider infinite line picture imposing the condition $p_{i+N}=p_i$ and $q_{i+N}= q_{i} + L$ where $L$ is the total length. The potential $V$ is an arbitrary interaction, where particles interact only with the nearest neighbor sites.
L000758	
L000759	The standard way to construct the hydrodynamics is to employ the coarse-graining in space. As in Ref. \cite{saito2021microscopic}, one may introduce the coarse-graining blocks where we assign the new coordinate $x$ containing $\ell_{\rm cg}$ numbers of particles. We focus only on the three conserved quantities out of totally $2N$ degrees of freedoms, i.e., local stretch arising from the conservation of the system size, local momentum, and local energy. This coarse-graining procedure is mathematically equivalent to project the microscopic Newtonian dynamics onto the three conserved quantities. This procedure is performed through Zwanzig's projection operator method (See, e.g., \cite{zwanzig,saito2021microscopic,zubarevmorozof}). Due to this projection, degrees of freedoms other than local conserved quantities play a role of thermal noise, where near equilibrium the fluctuation dissipation theorem should hold.
L000760	
L000761	Based on the above procedure, we discuss the general aspect of the local conserved quantities. Let $l(x,t)$, $g(x,t)$ and $e(x,t)$ be coarse-garined local stretch, momentum, and energy density. We now consider consider small fluctuations around thermal equilibrium,
L000762	\begin{align}
L000763	l(x,t) &= l_{\mathrm{eq}} + \delta l(x,t), \\
L000764	g(x,t) &= g_{\mathrm{eq}} + \delta g(x,t), \\
L000765	e(x,t) &= e_{\mathrm{eq}} + \delta e(x,t),
L000766	\end{align}
L000767	which we collectively denote by $\bm{u} = (\delta l, \delta g, \delta e)$. From the continuity equation, the local conserved quantities are connected to the local currents as
L000768	\begin{align}
L000769	\partial_t \bm{u} + \partial_x {\cal J} = \bm{0} \, ,
L000770	\end{align}
L000771	where ${\cal J} = ({\cal J}_l, {\cal J}_g, {\cal J}_e)$ is the corresponding currents. From the projection picture explained above, the local currents should have the fluctuating part in addition to the deterministic part as
L000772	\begin{align}
L000773	{\cal J} = {\cal J}^{\mathrm{det}} + {\cal J}^{\mathrm{ran}}\, ,
L000774	\end{align}
L000775	where the fluctuating part ${\cal J}^{\mathrm{ran}}$ is modeled as Gaussian white noise with correlation
L000776	\begin{align}
L000777	    \langle {\cal J}_a^\mathrm{ran}(x,t) {\cal J}_b^\mathrm{ran}(x',t')\rangle = 2B_{ab}\delta(t-t')\delta(x-x'),
L000778	\end{align}
L000779	Here, $B$ is a symmetric matrix determined by symmetry.
L000780	
L000781	
L000782	
L000783	\subsection{Gradient expansion}
L000784	
L000785	The deterministic part ${\cal J}_{\mathrm{det}}$ is expanded in $\bm{u}$ to second order:
L000786	\begin{align}
L000787	    {\cal J}_a^\mathrm{det}&(x,t)=\sum_{b}\int dy\tilde{A}_{ab}(x-y) u_b(y,t)+\sum_{b,c}\int dydz \tilde{H}^a_{bc}(x-y,x-z)u_b(y,t)u_c(z,t)+O(u^3).
L000788	\end{align}
L000789	The expansion coefficients are generally nonlocal, but in the long-wavelength limit we expand $\tilde{A}$ and $\tilde{H}$ in spatial derivatives:
L000790	\begin{align}
L000791	\tilde{A}_{ab}(x-y) &\sim \biggl(A_{ab} - D_{ab} \partial_x + O(\partial_x^2)\biggr) \delta(x-y), \\
L000792	\tilde{H}^a_{bc}(x-y, x-z) &\sim \biggl(H^a_{bc} + O(\partial_x)\biggr) \delta(x-y) \delta(x-z).
L000793	\end{align}
L000794	Higher-order derivatives are irrelevant at long distances.
L000795	The resulting fluctuating hydrodynamic equation is
L000796	\begin{align}
L000797	\sum_{b} \biggl(\delta_{ab} \partial_t + A_{ab}  \partial_x - D_{ab}  \partial_x^2\biggr) u_b + \sum_{b,c} H^a_{bc}  \partial_x (u_b u_c)
L000798	+ \partial_x  {\cal J}_a^\mathrm{ran} = 0. \label{EOM original}
L000799	\end{align}
L000800	Note that the expansion is truncated at the relevant orders in the long-time and long-distance scaling limit, as discussed in Sec.~\ref{scaling analysis}.
L000801	
L000802	\subsection{Equilibrium conditions}
L000803	\label{Derivation of equilibrium conditions}
L000804	We consider the isolated system described by the Hamiltonian (\ref{supplhamil}). Nevertheless,
L000805	it is expected that the local conserved quantities exhibit thermalization, where the statistics of the local quantities is well described by the equilibrium distribution. The intensive variables such as effective temperature are determined through the initial condition.
L000806	
L000807	From this picture, we impose  a fundamental requirement associated with the thermal equilibrium. In our model, the equilibrium distribution is assumed to be
L000808	\begin{align}
L000809	\Pfp_{\mathrm{eq}}[u] &= \frac{1}{Z} \exp\left[-\frac{1}{2} \sum_{a,b} \int dx  u_a(x)  \Co_{ab}^{-1}  u_b(x)\right]\label{Peq}, \\
L000810	\Co^{-1} &= \begin{pmatrix}
L000811	a_1 & 0 & a_0 \\
L000812	0 & 1 & 0 \\
L000813	a_0 & 0 & a_3
L000814	\end{pmatrix}, \label{inverseC}
L000815	\end{align}
L000816	where the parameters $a_i$ are determined by the thermodynamic state, and the zeros arise from time-reversal symmetry. From the requirement that the Fokker–Planck equation associated with Eq.~(\ref{EOM original}) admits the distribution (\ref{Peq}) as a solution, we obtain the following conditions:
L000817	\begin{align}
L000818	D \Co =& B, \label{eq:FDT1}\\
L000819	A \Co  =& \Co A^T, \label{eq:FDT2}\\
L000820	\iCH^a_{bc} =&\iCH^b_{ca}=\iCH^c_{ab}. \label{potential condition}
L000821	\end{align}
L000822	where we have introduced $\iCH^a_{bc}=\sum_d\Co^{-1}_{ad}H^d_{bc}$.
L000823	
L000824	
L000825	Below, we derive the equilibrium conditions (\ref{eq:FDT1})-(\ref{potential condition}).
L000826	The Fokker-Planck equation corresponding to Eq.~(\ref{EOM original}) is given by
L000827	\begin{align}
L000828	    \partial_t \Pfp&+\sum_{a,b}\int dx \biggl(\partial_x\frac{\delta}{\delta u_a}\biggr) \biggl[A_{ab}u_b+\sum_cH^a_{bc}u_bu_c-D_{ab}\partial_xu_b-
L000829	    B_{ab} \biggl(\partial_x\frac{\delta}{\delta u_{b}}\biggr)\biggr]\Pfp=0,
L000830	\end{align}
L000831	where $\Pfp[\bm{u}]$ is the probability distribution function.
L000832	The detailed balance condition reads
L000833	\begin{align}
L000834	\biggl[-D_{ab}\partial_xu_b-
L000835	
L000836	B_{ab}
L000837	\biggl(\partial_x\frac{\delta}{\delta u_{b}}\biggr)\biggr]\Pfp_{\mathrm{eq}}=0.
L000838	\end{align}
L000839	When the equilibrium distribution is given by the form (\ref{Peq}), we obtain Eq.~(\ref{eq:FDT1}).
L000840	
L000841	In addition to the detailed balance, the following condition remains for the Fokker–Planck equation to admit the equilibrium distribution as a solution:
L000842	\begin{align}
L000843	   \sum_{a,b}\int dx \biggl(\partial_x\frac{\delta}{\delta u_a}\biggr)
L000844	   \biggl(A_{ab}u_b+\sum_cH^a_{bc}u_bu_c\biggr)\Pfp_{\mathrm{eq}}=0,
L000845	\end{align}
L000846	for arbitrary $u_a(x)$.
L000847	This condition can be decomposed order by order in $u_a$, leading to the following relations
L000848	\begin{align}
L000849	  \sum_{a,b}\int dx \biggl(\partial_x\frac{\delta}{\delta u_a}\biggr)
L000850	   A_{ab}u_b\Pfp_{\mathrm{eq}}&=-\sum_{a,b,c}\int dx
L000851	   (\partial_xu_c)\Co^{-1}_{ca}A_{ab}u_b\Pfp_{\mathrm{eq}}=0,\label{A condition}\\
L000852	        \sum_{a,b,c}\int dx \biggl(\partial_x\frac{\delta}{\delta u_a}\biggr) H^a_{bc}u_bu_c\Pfp_{\mathrm{eq}}&= -\sum_{a,b,c,d}\int dx  (\partial_xu_d)\Co^{-1}_{da}H^a_{bc}u_bu_c\Pfp_{\mathrm{eq}}=0 \label{H condition}.
L000853	\end{align}
L000854	
L000855	After dividing by $\Pfp_{\mathrm{eq}}$, we obtain
L000856	\begin{align}
L000857	\sum_{b,c}\int dx \, (\partial_x u_c) K_{cb} u_b &= 0, \label{A condition2} \\
L000858	\sum_{b,c,d}\int dx \, (\partial_x u_d) \iCH^d_{bc} u_b u_c &= 0,
L000859	\label{H condition2}
L000860	\end{align}
L000861	where we have introduced the matrix $K := \Co^{-1} A$.
L000862	
L000863	We first derive Eq.~(\ref{eq:FDT2}) from Eq.~(\ref{A condition2}).
L000864	To this end, we decompose $K$ into its symmetric and antisymmetric parts,
L000865	\begin{align}
L000866	K = K^S + K^A,
L000867	\end{align}
L000868	where $(K^S)^T = K^S$ and $(K^A)^T = -K^A$.
L000869	For the symmetric part $K^S$, we obtain
L000870	\begin{align}
L000871	\sum_{b,c}\int dx \, (\partial_x u_c) K^S_{cb} u_b
L000872	&= \frac{1}{2} \sum_{b,c}\int dx \, \partial_x \!\left( u_c K^S_{cb} u_b \right) \\
L000873	&= \frac{1}{2} \sum_{b,c} \Bigl[ u_c K^S_{cb} u_b \Bigr]_{\text{boundary}} \\
L000874	&= 0 ,
L000875	\end{align}
L000876	where we have imposed periodic boundary conditions, under which the boundary contributions vanish.
L000877	Next, the antisymmetric part $K^A$ must satisfy
L000878	\begin{align}
L000879	\sum_{b,c}\int dx \, (\partial_x u_c) K^A_{cb} u_b = 0 .
L000880	\end{align}
L000881	Since this condition must hold for arbitrary periodic functions $u_a(x)$, it follows that $K^A = 0$.
L000882	Therefore, we obtain
L000883	\begin{align}
L000884	\Co^{-1} A = (\Co^{-1} A)^T .
L000885	\end{align}
L000886	Noting that $\Co^T = \Co$, we arrive at Eq.~(\ref{eq:FDT2}).
L000887	
L000888	
L000889	To derive Eq.~(\ref{potential condition}), we introduce the functional
L000890	\begin{align}
L000891	F^a[u] := \sum_{b,c} \iCH^a_{bc} u_b u_c .
L000892	\end{align}
L000893	Using periodic boundary conditions, Eq.~(\ref{H condition2}) can be rewritten as
L000894	\begin{align}
L000895	\sum_a \int dx \, (\partial_x u_a) F^a[u]
L000896	&= \sum_a \oint du_a \, F^a[u] \\
L000897	&= 0 .
L000898	\end{align}
L000899	Since the integral over any closed path in $u$-space vanishes, the one-form $\sum_a F^a[u] \, du_a$ must be exact. Consequently, we obtain
L000900	\begin{align}
L000901	\frac{\delta F^a[u]}{\delta u_b}
L000902	=
L000903	\frac{\delta F^b[u]}{\delta u_a}.
L000904	\end{align}
L000905	Using this relation together with $\iCH^a_{bc} = \iCH^a_{cb}$, we arrive at Eq.~(\ref{potential condition}).
L000906	
L000907	\section{Symmetry-based fluctuating hydrodynamics}
L000908	In this section, we derive the symmetry-based fluctuating hydrodynamics
L000909	by imposing covariance on the hydrodynamic equation~(\ref{EOM original})
L000910	under a set of symmetries, which leads to constraints on the parameters of the equations.
L000911	By combining these constraints with the equilibrium conditions~(\ref{eq:FDT1})–(\ref{potential condition}),
L000912	we obtain the symmetry-based fluctuating hydrodynamics.
L000913	
L000914	\subsection{General case}
L000915	\label{sec:generalcase}
L000916	Here, we determine the expansion coefficients $A$, $B$, $D$, and $H$ solely from conservation laws and symmetry considerations. The stretch variables are directly connected to the local momentum (see, e.g., \cite{saito2021microscopic}), i.e.,
L000917	\begin{align}
L000918	\partial_t u_1 - \partial_x u_2 &= 0 \, . \label{u1u2prop}
L000919	\end{align}
L000920	Hence, we find
L000921	\begin{align}
L000922	A_{1a} &= (0, -1, 0), \label{supple_a1}\\
L000923	{\cal J}_{\mathrm{ran}}^1(x,t)&=0,\\
L000924	D_{1a} &= B_{1a} = (0, 0, 0), \\
L000925	H^1_{ab} &= \mathcal{O}, \label{supple_h1}
L000926	\end{align}
L000927	where $\mathcal{O}$ denotes the zero matrix.
L000928	
L000929	We next discuss time-reversal symmetry. The  $A$ and $D$ terms correspond to time-reversible and time-irreversible contributions, respectively. The $H$ term is time-reversible due to thermodynamic stability.
L000930	Under time reversal transformation
L000931	\begin{align}
L000932	t \to -t, \quad u_1 \to u_1,\quad  u_2 \to -u_2,\quad u_3 \to u_3,   \label{tr_ope}
L000933	\end{align}
L000934	reversible terms transform in the same way as the time-derivative term, while irreversible terms transform with the opposite sign.
L000935	To satisfy time-reversal covariance, several matrix elements must vanish, and the coefficients can be generically written as
L000936	\begin{align}
L000937	A &= \begin{pmatrix}
L000938	0 & -1 & 0 \\
L000939	A_{21} & 0 & A_{23} \\
L000940	0 & A_{32} & 0
L000941	\end{pmatrix}, \label{A matrix 0} \\
L000942	D &= \begin{pmatrix}
L000943	0 & 0 & 0 \\
L000944	0 & D_{22} & 0 \\
L000945	D_{31} & 0 & D_{33}
L000946	\end{pmatrix}, \label{D matrix 0}\\
L000947	H^2 &= \begin{pmatrix}
L000948	H^2_{11} & 0 & H^2_{13} \\
L000949	0 & H^2_{22} & 0 \\
L000950	H^2_{13} & 0 & H^2_{33}
L000951	\end{pmatrix}, \\
L000952	H^3 &= \begin{pmatrix}
L000953	0 & H^3_{12} & 0 \\
L000954	H^3_{12} & 0 & H^3_{23} \\
L000955	0 & H^3_{23} & 0
L000956	\end{pmatrix}.
L000957	\end{align}
L000958	From Eqs.~(\ref{inverseC}), (\ref{eq:FDT1}) and (\ref{D matrix 0}), we obtain
L000959	\begin{align}
L000960	D &= \begin{pmatrix}
L000961	0 & 0 & 0 \\
L000962	0 & D_{22} & 0 \\
L000963	(a_0 / a_3) D_{33} & 0 & D_{33}
L000964	\end{pmatrix}, \label{D matrix} \\
L000965	B &=\begin{pmatrix}
L000966	0 & 0 & 0 \\
L000967	0 &  D_{22} & 0 \\
L000968	0 & 0 & D_{33} / a_3
L000969	\end{pmatrix}.\label{B matrix}
L000970	\end{align}
L000971	From  Eqs.~(\ref{inverseC}), (\ref{eq:FDT2}) and (\ref{A matrix 0}), we get the relations
L000972	\begin{align}
L000973	A_{21} = -a_1 + a_0 A_{32}, \quad A_{23} = -a_0 + a_3 A_{32}.
L000974	\end{align}
L000975	Defining $P = A_{32}$, we write
L000976	\begin{align}
L000977	A = \begin{pmatrix}
L000978	0 & -1 & 0 \\
L000979	a_0 P - a_1 & 0 & a_3 P - a_0 \\
L000980	0 & P & 0
L000981	\end{pmatrix}.\label{A matirx}
L000982	\end{align}
L000983	Finally, the condition (\ref{potential condition}) provides the relations
L000984	\begin{align}
L000985	H^2_{11} &= a_0 H^3_{12},\label{pot cond1} \\
L000986	H^2_{33} &= a_3 H^3_{23}, \\
L000987	H^2_{13} &= a_3 H^3_{12}, \\
L000988	H^2_{13} &= a_0 H^3_{23}. \label{pot cond4}
L000989	\end{align}
L000990	These relations reduce the number of independent components of $H$ to two, namely $H^2_{11}$ and $H^2_{22}$. We thus obtain
L000991	\begin{align}
L000992	H^2 &= \begin{pmatrix}
L000993	\Hgll & 0 & (a_3/a_0) \Hgll \\
L000994	0 & \Hggg & 0 \\
L000995	(a_3/a_0) \Hgll & 0 & (a_3^2 / a_0^2) \Hgll
L000996	\end{pmatrix},\label{H2 matrix} \\
L000997	H^3 &= \begin{pmatrix}
L000998	0 & (1/a_0) \Hgll & 0 \\
L000999	(1/a_0) \Hgll & 0 & (a_3/a_0^2) \Hgll \\
L001000	0 & (a_3/a_0^2) \Hgll & 0
L001001	\end{pmatrix}. \label{H3 matrix}
L001002	\end{align}
L001003	
L001004	\subsection{Special case with the space-inversion symmetry}
L001005	Here, we consider the special case where the Hamiltonian has the space-inversion symmetry, $i \to N-i$. This case is special, since this symmetry is rarely realized in realistic materials. In addition to Eqs.(\ref{eq:FDT1})-(\ref{potential condition}), (\ref{tr_ope}), and (\ref{supple_a1})-(\ref{supple_h1}), we need to take account of the covariance of the fluctuating hydrodynamics under the transformation
L001006	\begin{align}
L001007	 x \to -x, \quad u_1 \to -u_1, \quad u_2 \to u_2,\quad  u_3 \to u_3.  \label{supp_invs}
L001008	\end{align}
L001009	This covariance imposes additional constraints, forbidding certain components of the coefficients.
L001010	The coefficient matrices with space-inversion symmetry take the form
L001011	\begin{align}
L001012	\Co^{-1} &=  \begin{pmatrix}
L001013	a_1 & 0 & 0 \\
L001014	0 & 1 & 0 \\
L001015	0 & 0 & a_3
L001016	\end{pmatrix}, \label{Cinverse matirx symm}\\
L001017	A &= \begin{pmatrix}
L001018	0 & -1 & 0 \\
L001019	- a_1 & 0 & 0 \\
L001020	0 & 0 & 0
L001021	\end{pmatrix}, \label{A matirx symm}\\
L001022	D &= \begin{pmatrix}
L001023	0 & 0 & 0 \\
L001024	0 & D_{22} & 0 \\
L001025	0 & 0 & D_{33}
L001026	\end{pmatrix}, \label{D matrix symm}\\
L001027	B &=\begin{pmatrix}
L001028	0 & 0 & 0 \\
L001029	0 & D_{22} & 0 \\
L001030	0 & 0 & D_{33} / a_3
L001031	\end{pmatrix}, \label{B matrix symm}\\
L001032	H^1 &= H^2 = H^3 = \mathcal{O}.
L001033	\end{align}
L001034	We emphasize that all the $H$ matrices vanish under space-inversion symmetry.
L001035	
L001036	We show that any orders of nonlinear coefficients vanish under space-inversion symmetry. Consider the deterministic current of the form
L001037	\begin{align}
L001038	{\cal J}^{\rm det}_{a} &= \sum_b A_{ab} u_b + \sum_{b_1, \cdots , b_n} H^a_{b_1 , \cdots , b_n}  u_{b_1} \cdots u_{b_n} \, ,
L001039	\end{align}
L001040	where $H^a_{b_1 , \cdots , b_n}$ is the $n$th order nonlinear coefficient which is symmetric under any exchange between $b_i$ and $b_j$, i.e., $H^a_{b_1, \cdots, b_i \cdots, b_j , \cdots b_n}=H^a_{b_1, \cdots, b_j \cdots, b_i , \cdots b_n}$. In this case, through the same procedure as in Sec.\ref{Derivation of equilibrium conditions}, Eq.(\ref{potential condition}) in the equilibrium condition is replaced by
L001041	\begin{align}
L001042	\hat{H}_{c,(b_1, \cdots , b_n)} &=\hat{H}_{b_j, (b_1,\cdots, c, \cdots, b_n)} \, , ~~~\forall j
L001043	\label{ncyclic} \, ,
L001044	\end{align}
L001045	where
L001046	\begin{align}
L001047	\hat{H}_{c,(b_1, \cdots , b_n)} &:=\sum_{a} ({\Co}^{-1})_{c a} H^{a}_{b_1, \cdots, b_n} \, .
L001048	\end{align}
L001049	Note that the matrix ${\Co}^{-1}$ is diagonal as in (\ref{Cinverse matirx symm}) and hence $\hat{H}_{c,(b_1, \cdots , b_n)}$ is proportional to $H^{a}_{b_1, \cdots, b_n}$ as
L001050	\begin{align}
L001051	\hat{H}_{c,(b_1, \cdots, b_n)} &=\left\{
L001052	\begin{array} {ll}
L001053	0 & c=1 \, \\
L001054	H^{2}_{b_1, \cdots, b_n} & c=2 \, \\
L001055	a_3 H^{3}_{b_1, \cdots, b_n} & c=3 \,
L001056	\end{array}
L001057	\right.  \, , \label{hhatb1bn}
L001058	\end{align}
L001059	where we use the property (\ref{u1u2prop}) for $c=1$. If $(b_1, \cdots , b_n)$ contains $1$, the condition (\ref{ncyclic}) leads to $H^{a}_{b_1, \cdots, b_n}=0$ due to the expression (\ref{hhatb1bn}). When $(b_1, \cdots , b_n)$ does not contain $1$, the symmetry (\ref{supp_invs}) requires $H^{a}_{b_1, \cdots, b_n}=-H^{a}_{b_1, \cdots, b_n}$, which implies $0$. Hence, we conclude that all higher order coefficients must vanish in the dynamics.
