L001783	\subsection{System size}
L001784	We first examine the effect of the system size $L$ on the time correlation $\Sf^\alpha(x=0,t)$ in Fig.~\ref{supfig3}.
L001785	As discussed in Sec.~\ref{sec:finite-size-correction}, the finite-size effect arising from the conservation law can be corrected by subtracting $C_{\alpha}$.
L001786	However, a residual finite-size effect persists due to the periodic boundary conditions.
L001787	This boundary effect becomes significant at a characteristic time $t_c \approx L/(2c_s)$, which corresponds to the time required for counter-propagating sound waves to meet.
L001788	Consequently, as shown in Figs.~\ref{supfig3}(a) and (c), the correlation deviates from the power-law decay and saturates at long times.
L001789	The bottom panels [Figs.~\ref{supfig3}(b) and (d)] show the extracted exponent $z$.
L001790	As $L$ increases, the time window in which $z=3/2$ (i.e., $\propto t^{-2/3}$) widens.
L001791	This confirms that the RG prediction $z=3/2$ is recovered in the $L \to \infty$ limit.
L001792	The size $L=8192$ used in the main text provides a characteristic time of $t_c \approx 40960$ (for $c_s=0.1$), which is sufficiently large to observe the scaling behavior before the finite-size effect becomes dominant.
