L001896	\section{Remarks on the original nonlinear fluctuating hydrodynamics}
L001897	We reexamine the nonlinear fluctuating hydrodynamics (NFH) theory proposed in Ref.~\cite{spohn2014nonlinear}, which can be written in the same form as Eq.~(\ref{EOM original}) but with the following coefficients:
L001898	\begin{align}
L001899	A &= \begin{pmatrix}
L001900	     0 & -1 & 0\\
L001901	    \partial_l P & 0 & \partial_e P\\
L001902	    0 & P & 0
L001903	\end{pmatrix}, \label{Spohn A matrix} \\
L001904	D
L001905	&= \begin{pmatrix}
L001906	0 & 0 & 0 \\
L001907	0 & D_u & 0 \\
L001908	\tilde{D}_e & 0 & D_e
L001909	\end{pmatrix}, \\
L001910	H^2 &=\frac{1}{2} \begin{pmatrix}
L001911	\partial_l^2 P & 0 & \partial_l\partial_e P\\
L001912	0 & \partial_e P & 0 \\
L001913	\partial_l\partial_e P& 0 & \partial_e^2 P
L001914	\end{pmatrix},\\
L001915	H^3 &= \frac{1}{2}\begin{pmatrix}
L001916	0 & \partial_l\partial_e P & 0 \\
L001917	\partial_l\partial_e P & 0 & \partial_lP \\
L001918	0 & \partial_lP & 0
L001919	\end{pmatrix},
L001920	\end{align}
L001921	and $H^1=\mathcal{O}$ where $\mathcal{O}$ is the zero matrix.
L001922	Here, $P$ is the pressure and  $D_u$, $D_e$, and $\tilde{D}_e$  are the diffusion constants.
L001923	The coefficients $B$  are determined to satisfy the equilibrium condition ($\ref{eq:FDT1}$).
L001924	We note that all components of $H$ are expressed in terms of thermodynamic derivatives.
L001925	However, as we show below, this formulation fails to satisfy the equilibrium condition~(\ref{potential condition}) for general equilibrium states.
L001926	
L001927	By imposing Eq.~(\ref{potential condition}) on $H$,
L001928	we obtain the following constraints on the thermodynamic derivatives:
L001929	\begin{align}
L001930	    \partial_l^2 P &= a_0 \partial_l P,\quad \partial_e^2P = a_3 \partial_eP, \\
L001931	    \partial_l\partial_e P &= a_3 \partial_lP, \quad
L001932	    \partial_l\partial_e P = a_0 \partial_eP. \label{eq:potential cond Spohn}
L001933	\end{align}
L001934	These additional constraints on the thermodynamic quantities indicate that the original NFH theory proposed in Ref.~\cite{spohn2014nonlinear} does not satisfy the thermalization condition for general equilibrium states.
L001935	These relations cannot be satisfied simultaneously for general $P(l,e)$, indicating that the formulation is physically inconsistent.
L001936	
L001937	
L001938	
L001939	
L001940	Furthermore, these constraints lead to an unphysical situation, as shown below.
L001941	From Eq.~(\ref{eq:potential cond Spohn}), we obtain
L001942	\begin{align}
L001943	    a_3\,\partial_l P = a_0\,\partial_e P. \label{eq:pot cond Spohn 2}
L001944	\end{align}
L001945	To  express the thermodynamic derivatives in terms of the parameters $a_i$,
L001946	
L001947	we next follow the formulation adopted in Ref.~\cite{spohn2014nonlinear}.
L001948	Specifically, we summarize its construction based on the local Gibbs distribution for the conserved quantities written in terms of the microscopic variables $r_i = q_{i+1} - q_i$, $p_i$, and $\epsilon_i = p_i^2/(2m) + V(r_i)$.
L001949	We collectively denote these by $\mathcal{U}_{a,i} = (r_i, p_i, \epsilon_i)$, and write the distribution as
L001950	\begin{align}
L001951	    \Pfp_{\mathrm{LG}}[\mathcal{U}_{a,i}]
L001952	    = \frac{1}{Z_{\mathrm{LG}}}
L001953	    \exp\biggl[-\sum_{a,i} \lambda^a_{i}\, \mathcal{U}_{a,i}\biggr],
L001954	\end{align}
L001955	where $\lambda^a_i$ are the local intensive variables.
L001956	Note that this definition of the hydrodynamic fields---particularly the stretch---differs from ours, but we follow this convention here for consistency with Ref.~\cite{spohn2014nonlinear}.
L001957	At global equilibrium, they take constant values
L001958	\begin{align}
L001959	    \bm{\lambda}_{\mathrm{eq}} = (\beta P,\, 0,\, \beta),
L001960	\end{align}
L001961	with $\beta$ being the inverse temperature.
L001962	The hydrodynamic fields $u_a(x)$ in Ref.~\cite{spohn2014nonlinear} are then defined  as the local deviations of these conserved quantities from their equilibrium averages,
L001963	\begin{align}
L001964	    u_a(x)
L001965	    := \langle \mathcal{U}_{a,i}\rangle_{\mathrm{LG}}
L001966	    - \langle \mathcal{U}_{a,i}\rangle_{\mathrm{eq}}.
L001967	\end{align}
L001968	The pressure $P$ is regarded as a thermodynamic function of the conserved quantities $u_a$,
L001969	and its derivatives are related to the linear response of the intensive variables.
L001970	Specifically, using
L001971	\begin{align}
L001972	    \frac{\partial \lambda^a}{\partial u_b} = -\Co^{-1}_{ab}, \label{eq:dlam_du}
L001973	\end{align}
L001974	which follows directly from the definition of the covariance matrix in the local Gibbs distribution, we find
L001975	\begin{align}
L001976	    \partial_l P
L001977	    &= \frac{1}{\beta}\biggl(\frac{\partial(\beta P)}{\partial l} - P\, \frac{\partial \beta}{\partial l}\biggr), \nonumber \\
L001978	    &= \frac{1}{\beta}\biggl(\frac{\partial \lambda^1}{\partial u_1} - P\, \frac{\partial \lambda^3}{\partial u_1} \biggr), \nonumber \\
L001979	    &= \beta^{-1}\!\left(-\Co^{-1}_{11} + P\, \Co^{-1}_{13}\right), \nonumber \\
L001980	    &= \beta^{-1}\!\left(a_0 P - a_1\right), \\
L001981	    \partial_e P
L001982	    &= \beta^{-1}\!\left(a_3 P - a_0\right).
L001983	\end{align}
L001984	Using these expressions, the constraint~(\ref{eq:pot cond Spohn 2}) reduces to
L001985	\begin{align}
L001986	    a_0^2 - a_1 a_3 = 0.
L001987	\end{align}
L001988	This relation implies a singular equilibrium distribution with $\det \Co^{-1} = 0$.
L001989	Therefore, the NFH theory proposed in Ref.~\cite{spohn2014nonlinear} satisfies the equilibrium condition only in a singular, hence unphysical, state.
L001990	We note that the $A$ matrix in Ref.~\cite{spohn2014nonlinear} satisfies the equilibrium condition~(\ref{eq:FDT2}) without any additional constraints and coincides with that obtained in our derivation.
L001991	
L001992	Furthermore, the $G$ matrix in Ref.~\cite{spohn2014nonlinear} is characterized by the following symmetry relations:
L001993	\begin{align}
L001994	G^\alpha_{\beta \gamma}&=G^\alpha_{\gamma \beta}, \quad
L001995	G^\sigma_{\beta \gamma}=-G^{-\sigma}_{-\beta -\gamma}, \quad
L001996	G^+_{0 +}=G^+_{0 -}, \nonumber\\
L001997	G^0_{++}&=-G^0_{--}, \quad
L001998	G^0_{\beta\gamma}=0 \; \text{(otherwise)},
L001999	\end{align}
L002000	where $\sigma=\pm$. The parametrized expressions are given by
L002001	\begin{align}
L002002	    G^+&=\begin{pmatrix}
L002003	        \nu_1 & \nu_2 & \nu_3\\
L002004	        \nu_2 & \nu_4 & \nu_2  \\
L002005	        \nu_3 & \nu_2  &\nu_5
L002006	    \end{pmatrix}, \,~
L002007	       G^-=\begin{pmatrix}
L002008	        -\nu_5 &  -\nu_2 & -\nu_3\\
L002009	        -\nu_2  & -\nu_4 & -\nu_2 \\
L002010	        -\nu_3 &-\nu_2& -\nu_1
L002011	    \end{pmatrix}, \, ~
L002012	        G^0=\begin{pmatrix}
L002013	        \nu_0 & 0 & 0\\
L002014	         0 & 0 & 0 \\
L002015	        0 & 0& -\nu_0
L002016	    \end{pmatrix}.
L002017	\end{align}
L002018	Using this $G$ in the RG analysis, however, we did not obtain a nontrivial fixed point other than the trivial EW one when solving the RG equations numerically with Mathematica.
L002019	This result indicates that the NFH proposed in Ref.~\cite{spohn2014nonlinear} fails to capture the nontrivial fixed-point structure responsible for the KPZ universality class.
L002020	We also performed extensive numerical computations for this parameter setting and found that most parameter choices led to numerically unstable time evolutions.
L002021	Even for numerically stable parameter regimes, compared to our model, it remains unclear whether both the heat and sound modes exhibit the scaling behavior~\cite{DR}.
